import{H as e,at as t,it as n,vt as r}from"./chunks/framework.dmHZ7NHG.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"ARTS — Sealed Beauty & Harmony","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/arts.md","filePath":"docs/arts.md"}`),a={name:`docs/arts.md`};function o(e,i,a,o,s,c){return r(),n(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="arts-—-sealed-beauty-harmony" tabindex="-1">ARTS — Sealed Beauty &amp; Harmony <a class="header-anchor" href="#arts-—-sealed-beauty-harmony" aria-label="Permalink to “ARTS — Sealed Beauty &amp; Harmony”">​</a></h1><p><strong>The Framework:</strong> Art domains where the mathematics is sealed. Not subjective taste — objective ratios, harmonies, and structures that humans find beautiful.</p><p>Each art in trinity structure:</p><ol><li><strong>SEALED</strong> — proof of the ratio/harmony/structure (exact arithmetic)</li><li><strong>HONEST BOUNDARY</strong> — what the theorem cannot prove (why this ratio feels beautiful)</li><li><strong>AESTHETIC PAIR</strong> — the opening question (does math explain art or reduce it?)</li></ol><hr><h2 id="the-domains" tabindex="-1">The Domains <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-domains" aria-label="Permalink to “The Domains”">​</a></h2><h3 id="colour-8-theorems" tabindex="-1">Colour (8 theorems) <a class="header-anchor" href="#colour-8-theorems" aria-label="Permalink to “Colour (8 theorems)”">​</a></h3><p><strong>Sealed:</strong> The colour wheel is ℤ/12 (12 hues, wrapping). Complementary hues sit opposite (+6 half-turn, self-inverse involution). Triads are thirds (+4 landing {0,4,8}). Squares are fourths (+3 landing {0,3,6,9}). True colour is 8 bits a channel (2^24 = 16.7M colours).</p><p><strong>Honest Boundary:</strong> No proof that complementary colours are &quot;beautiful&quot; — only that they are symmetric.</p><p><strong>Aesthetic Question:</strong> <em>Does the colour wheel explain why red and green clash beautifully, or is math just a description?</em></p><h3 id="music-production-10-theorems" tabindex="-1">Music &amp; Production (10 theorems) <a class="header-anchor" href="#music-production-10-theorems" aria-label="Permalink to “Music &amp; Production (10 theorems)”">​</a></h3><p><strong>Sealed:</strong> The chromatic scale is ℤ/12 (octave wraps). An octave doubles frequency (440→880 Hz). The circle of fifths is one cycle (7 semitones coprime to 12 visits all 12). Reversing a clip is self-inverse. Phase inversion (x ↦ −x) is self-inverse. Their fusion reverse-then-invert is itself an involution.</p><p><strong>Honest Boundary:</strong> No proof that a major chord is &quot;happy&quot; or minor &quot;sad&quot; — only that they have different ratios.</p><p><strong>Aesthetic Question:</strong> <em>If we understand the mathematics of harmony perfectly, does music lose its magic?</em></p><h3 id="typography-14-theorems" tabindex="-1">Typography (14 theorems) <a class="header-anchor" href="#typography-14-theorems" aria-label="Permalink to “Typography (14 theorems)”">​</a></h3><p><strong>Sealed:</strong> The printer&#39;s units close exactly (6 picas = 72 points = 1 inch). The em is the type&#39;s own square (12pt type has a 12pt em). The 3:4:5 rectangle is harmonious (its diagonal is whole: 3²+4²=5²). Fibonacci pages approach the golden ratio (5²−3·8 = 1). The readable measure is 66 characters (inside the 45–75 range typographers keep). A ream is 500 sheets (20 quires of 25).</p><p><strong>Honest Boundary:</strong> No proof that a 3:4:5 page is &quot;more beautiful&quot; than a 1:√2 page — only that it satisfies Pythagorean closure.</p><p><strong>Aesthetic Question:</strong> <em>Does knowing that a beautiful page follows the 3-4-5 triangle make it more or less beautiful?</em></p><h3 id="photography-8-theorems" tabindex="-1">Photography (8 theorems) <a class="header-anchor" href="#photography-8-theorems" aria-label="Permalink to “Photography (8 theorems)”">​</a></h3><p><strong>Sealed:</strong> Shutter speeds are powers of 2 (1, 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/16, 1/32, 1/64). Apertures are f-stops (f/1, f/1.4, f/2, etc.). The standard rounds: 1/125 ≈ 1/128, f/1.4 ≈ √2. Reciprocity is exact addition of stops (1 stop of shutter = 1 stop of aperture). ISO doublings are exact powers of 2 (100, 200, 400, 800, 1600, 3200).</p><p><strong>Honest Boundary:</strong> No proof that a &quot;correctly exposed&quot; photo is beautiful — only that it follows the photographic standard.</p><p><strong>Aesthetic Question:</strong> <em>Is a perfectly exposed photograph better than an imperfectly exposed one that moves you?</em></p><h3 id="the-spectrum-8-theorems" tabindex="-1">The Spectrum (8 theorems) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-spectrum-8-theorems" aria-label="Permalink to “The Spectrum (8 theorems)”">​</a></h3><p><strong>Sealed:</strong> Wavelength × frequency = <em>c</em> (light speed, exact). The visible window is one octave (700nm/400nm ≈ 1.75 &lt; 2). The SEVEN bands (radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray, gamma) ordered by frequency. Photon energy E = h·f.</p><p><strong>Honest Boundary:</strong> No proof that visible light is &quot;special&quot; — only that it&#39;s where evolution happened.</p><p><strong>Aesthetic Question:</strong> <em>Why does human vision peak in the green part of the spectrum? Is it accident or design?</em></p><hr><h2 id="creating-art-from-math" tabindex="-1">Creating Art from Math <a class="header-anchor" href="#creating-art-from-math" aria-label="Permalink to “Creating Art from Math”">​</a></h2><p>Each arts folder contains:</p><ol><li><strong>Proof</strong> — link to sealed theorem</li><li><strong>Interactive tool</strong> — explore the ratio/harmony (coming)</li><li><strong>Gallery</strong> — examples from nature and culture</li><li><strong>Paradox</strong> — the open question about math and beauty</li></ol><p><strong>The Honest Scope:</strong> These theorems describe STRUCTURE that humans find beautiful. They do NOT prove beauty or explain why your heart moves. Math is the <strong>language</strong> of art, not its <strong>soul</strong>.</p><hr><h2 id="why-arts-matter" tabindex="-1">Why Arts Matter <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-arts-matter" aria-label="Permalink to “Why Arts Matter”">​</a></h2><p>A sealed arts theorem proves:</p><ul><li><strong>Patterns exist</strong> — measurable, verifiable, recomputable</li><li><strong>Humans gravitate to them</strong> — the colour wheel, the golden ratio, consonant intervals</li><li><strong>Structure and beauty are linked</strong> — but not identical</li></ul><p>But it cannot prove:</p><ul><li><strong>Why</strong> we love these patterns (evolution? culture? accident?)</li><li><strong>Whether</strong> mathematical beauty is objective or projected</li><li><strong>What</strong> beauty <em>is</em></li></ul><p><strong>The paradox:</strong> The most mathematical art (architecture, music composition, photography) often feels the most alive. Yet explaining the math does not diminish the magic — it deepens it.</p>`,38)]])}var s=e(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};